#EXTM3U
# M4-01 matcher fixture (D-EPG-2). One row per strategy, per ambiguity rule
# and per <display-name> shape; the guide side is tests/fixtures/epg-match.xml
# and the expected counts are tests/fixtures/epg-match.json. Do not reorder:
# the playlist order decides which row wins a feed base, and the Multi row
# depends on it. Append, do not insert.
#
# The last six rows were added on 2026-10-03 for the review of the M4 round.
# "Noise Channel (1080p) [Geo-blocked]" and "Guide Marker Channel" are the
# two rows the SEARCH key cannot match -- one carries the markers on OUR
# side, the other on the GUIDE's -- so between them they make each of
# M4-01's two epg_name_key call sites revertible-red; the
# Number First and Lang rows are guides whose first <display-name> is not the
# name; Markup and Sink are M4-02 sinks (a <desc> with a child element, and a
# <desc> whose cap cut lands inside a URL's userinfo).
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Exact.us" group-title="Match",Exact Channel
http://stream.example.test/exact.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Suffix.us@SD" group-title="Match",Suffix Channel
http://stream.example.test/suffix.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Bare.us" group-title="Match",Bare Channel
http://stream.example.test/bare.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="namehit.xx" group-title="Match",Name Only Channel
http://stream.example.test/namehit.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="ambi1.xx" group-title="Ambiguous",Twin Channel
http://stream.example.test/ambi1.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="ambi2.xx" group-title="Ambiguous",Twin Channel
http://stream.example.test/ambi2.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="guideambi.xx" group-title="Ambiguous",Double Declared
http://stream.example.test/guideambi.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="none.xx" group-title="Match",No Guide Channel
http://stream.example.test/none.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 group-title="Match",Unkeyed Channel
http://stream.example.test/unkeyed.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Detail.us" group-title="Match",Detail Channel
http://stream.example.test/detail.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Case.US" group-title="Match",Case Channel
http://stream.example.test/case.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Clash.us" group-title="Match",Clash Channel
http://stream.example.test/clash.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Multi.us" group-title="Match",Multi Channel
http://stream.example.test/multi.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="noise.xx" group-title="Match",Noise Channel (1080p) [Geo-blocked]
http://stream.example.test/noise.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="numfirst.xx" group-title="Match",Number First Channel
http://stream.example.test/numfirst.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="lang.xx" group-title="Match",Lang Channel
http://stream.example.test/lang.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Markup.us" group-title="Match",Markup Channel
http://stream.example.test/markup.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Sink.us" group-title="Match",Sink Channel
http://stream.example.test/sink.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="gnoise.xx" group-title="Match",Guide Marker Channel
http://stream.example.test/gnoise.m3u8
#
# --- F-EPG-11, the ADDRESS strategy (added 2026-10-04) -------------------
# ADDR joins a guide channel id to the row that STREAMS it: an alphanumeric
# run of at least EPG_ADDR_MIN_TOKEN characters in the row's stream URL that
# equals a declared guide id, and only when that run belongs to exactly one
# row. The URL LAYOUTS below are copied from the installed iptv-org US list
# -- ".../plu-<24 hex>.m3u8" for 185 of its 1,453 rows, and
# "images.pluto.tv/channels/<24 hex>/colorLogoPNG.png" for the artwork -- so
# the shapes are real; the ids are synthetic, so no row here points at a
# channel that exists.
#
# The common real shape. The guide name deliberately does NOT match the
# playlist name, so the address is the only thing that can have matched it.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="Addr.us" group-title="Addr",Address Channel
http://jmp.example.test/plu-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0001.m3u8
# A guide id that is not hex. ADDR names no id SHAPE, which is what makes it
# provider-general; `globalfashionchannel` is a real 20-character token of
# the installed list.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrSlug.us" group-title="Addr",Slug Channel
http://cdn.example.test/live/globalfashionchannel/index.m3u8
# The id is in the LOGO and not in the stream, so it must NOT match: 7 of the
# 8 real rows of this shape stream from a different distributor than the
# artwork (Pluto artwork on Amagi, CloudFront and Google DAI addresses).
# Artwork provenance is not stream identity.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrLogo.us" tvg-logo="http://images.example.test/channels/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0002/colorLogoPNG.png" group-title="Addr",Artwork Channel
http://amg.example.test/vevo/index.m3u8
# A guide id SHORTER than the floor, sitting in a word addresses really
# carry: the whole collision guard. `playlist` is a token of 583 real rows.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrShort.us" group-title="Addr",Short Token Channel
http://cdn.example.test/playlist.m3u8
# Two rows streaming one address: ambiguous on OUR side, so neither matches
# and both are counted in addrDroppedPlaylist.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrDupA.us" group-title="Addr",Dup Address A
http://jmp.example.test/plu-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0003.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrDupB.us" group-title="Addr",Dup Address B
http://jmp.example.test/plu-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0003.m3u8
# CONSTRUCTED: one row whose address names TWO declared guide channels. 0 of
# the 1,453 real rows do. Equal ranks are first-come, so the declaration that
# streams a programme first takes the row and the second is contested, never
# merged into it.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrTwo.us" group-title="Addr",Two Address Channel
http://cdn.example.test/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0004/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0005/index.m3u8
# ADDR over NAME, which is the real Heartland shape and the measurement that
# set the ranking: the guide declares ONE channel, the broadcaster's own
# playout matches it by name, and the provider's playout of it carries its
# id. The id names the PLAYOUT; the name only names the brand.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrRank.us@Eastern" group-title="Addr",Rank Channel (720p)
http://broadcaster.example.test/eastern/index.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrRank.us@Web" group-title="Addr",Rank Channel Web
http://jmp.example.test/plu-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0006.m3u8
# ID over ADDR: this row's tvg-id IS one guide channel's id while its address
# names a different one. The DECLARED join must win over the deduced one.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0007" group-title="Addr",Id Over Address Channel
http://jmp.example.test/plu-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0008.m3u8
# ADDR over FEED: one guide id is this row's address token AND the next row's
# feed base. The frozen data cannot rank these two (FEED matched 0 rows
# there), so the order is pinned here instead of left to a comment.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrFeed.us" group-title="Addr",Address Over Feed Channel
http://cdn.example.test/feedbasetoken1/index.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="feedbasetoken1@SD" group-title="Addr",Feed Base Channel
http://cdn.example.test/feedbase/index.m3u8
# ADDR is case-SENSITIVE: a URL path is case-sensitive by the URL spec, so
# folding case would read an identity the address does not assert.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrCase.us" group-title="Addr",Case Token Channel
http://cdn.example.test/MixedCaseToken12/index.m3u8
# The LOOKUP ORDER between ID and ADDR, which the rank alone does not pin:
# one guide id is the first row's DECLARED tvg-id and a token of the second
# row's address, so whichever map is consulted first decides which row gets
# the channel. UNVERIFIED on the frozen data -- 0 of its 1,453 rows carry a
# tvg-id that the guide declares -- so this is an argued order, pinned here
# rather than left to a comment: writing tvg-id="<guide id>" is the playlist
# author SAYING this row is that channel.
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0009" group-title="Addr",Declared Id Channel
http://broadcaster.example.test/declared/index.m3u8
#EXTINF:-1 tvg-id="AddrOther.us" group-title="Addr",Other Address Channel
http://jmp.example.test/plu-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa0009.m3u8
